## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design real-time, multi-tenant data pipelines with correct deduplication, rate limiting, and safe cross-tenant isolation. It also probes for practical IAM scoping, dead-letter handling, and observability under burst traffic.

## Key Concepts

- Pub/Sub streaming ingestion, Apache Beam (Dataflow) processing
- Deduplication by tenant_id + event_id with windowing
- Per-tenant rate limiting using Redis
- External risk-score enrichment via internal API
- Multi-tenant BigQuery routing: analytics.tenants.{tenant}.events
- Dead-letter queues, exponential backoff retries
- Least-privilege IAM, monitoring, alerting, cost control

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-Beam-style dedup snippet
// Deduplicate by (tenant_id, event_id) using per-tenant state
class DedupFn {
  process(element) {
    const key = element.tenant_id + ':' + element.event_id;
    if (state.has(key)) return;
    state.add(key);
    yield element;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test per-tenant rate limiting and data skew at scale?
- How would you secure Redis and ensure tenant isolation for secrets?
- How would you handle late-arriving events that would violate dedup windows?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests event-driven reasoning, idempotent cross-region replication, and minimal IAM in GCP.

## Key Concepts
- Cloud Functions trigger on Cloud Storage finalize events
- Copy between buckets across regions, preserving path
- Idempotency via MD5 hash comparison
- Least privilege IAM and retry semantics
- Basic test plan including re-upload scenario

## Code Example
```javascript
const {Storage} = require('@google-cloud/storage');
const storage = new Storage();
const SRC = 'ingest-bucket';
const DST = 'dr-bucket';
exports.replicateToDR = async (event, context) => {
  const file = event.name;
  const srcFile = storage.bucket(SRC).file(file);
  // get MD5 from metadata
  const [md] = await srcFile.getMetadata().then(m => [m.md5Hash]);
  const dstFile = storage.bucket(DST).file(file);
  try {
    const [dstMeta] = await dstFile.getMetadata().catch(() => [null]);
    if (dstMeta && dstMeta.md5Hash === md) { return; }
    await srcFile.copy(dstFile);
  } catch (e) {
    // publish to Pub/Sub/log for alerting
    throw e;
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this for large files or many concurrent events?
- How would you monitor, alert, and test failure scenarios?

